---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Is Gloomhaven Worth It? An Honest Look Before You Commit"
  description="A heavy box, a long campaign and a real time commitment. Who is it actually for?"
  game="gloomhaven"
  gameTitle="Gloomhaven"
  category="Buying Guide"
  product="universal-player-tray"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Gloomhaven spent years at the very top of BoardGameGeek's rankings, which
    makes it sound like a safe purchase. It isn't — not because it's a bad game,
    but because it asks for more than almost anything else on your shelf, and
    plenty of copies end up half-played.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Yes, if you have a stable group who will meet regularly for a year and want deep tactical combat. No, if your gaming is spontaneous, your group varies, or your shelf and evenings are already full." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="1–4" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Per scenario" value="2.5–3.5 hrs" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Campaign" value="100+ hrs" icon="📅" />
    <QuickFact label="Weight" value="Very heavy" icon="🧠" />
  </div>

  <h2>The Verdict at a Glance</h2>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-emerald-800 mb-3">✅ Buy It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-emerald-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• You have the same one to three people available regularly</li>
        <li>• Tactical puzzle combat is what you want from a game</li>
        <li>• Long-term character progression appeals to you</li>
        <li>• You'd happily play solo or two-player</li>
        <li>• You have somewhere to store — ideally leave out — a very big box</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-xl border border-red-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-red-800 mb-3">❌ Skip It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-red-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• Your group changes from week to week</li>
        <li>• You mostly play one-off games on the night</li>
        <li>• Long setup will stop you suggesting it</li>
        <li>• You want a rich story — the combat is the star, not the plot</li>
        <li>• You haven't tried the system yet (start with Jaws of the Lion)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What It Gets Right</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The card system is genuinely brilliant</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Two cards, four halves, pick two — with your own hand size acting as the scenario timer. It produces hard, interesting decisions every single turn, and nothing else quite plays like it. <a href="/games/gloomhaven/how-gloomhaven-works/">Here's how it works</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Character variety is enormous</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Seventeen classes that play genuinely differently, most of them locked behind progress. Retiring a character to unlock a new one keeps a long campaign feeling fresh in a way that levelling alone never would.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">No dungeon master, no arguments</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Monsters follow published behaviour decks. There's no referee, no interpretation, and nobody has to sit out to run the bad guys. For a group that wants the tactical RPG feel without the RPG overhead, that's exactly right.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What It Gets Wrong</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The setup tax is brutal</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Finding map tiles, sorting monsters and laying out a scenario regularly takes longer than a whole game of something else. It's the single most common reason campaigns quietly stop — see <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-setup-and-organisation/">taming the box</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The story is thin for its length</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Scenario text is a few paragraphs read aloud before the fight. If you're expecting narrative to match the hundred-hour runtime, you'll be disappointed. The combat is the content.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It punishes irregular groups hard</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Characters level, retire and unlock. A player who misses three sessions comes back behind, and swapping people in and out mid-campaign is awkward at best. This is a game for a committed roster.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="The most common regret isn't about the game">
    People rarely regret Gloomhaven's design. They regret buying the biggest box
    before knowing whether their group would sustain a campaign. Jaws of the Lion
    answers that question for a fraction of the price — see
    <a href="/games/gloomhaven/gloomhaven-vs-jaws-of-the-lion/">the comparison</a>.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    Gloomhaven deserves its reputation. The card system is one of the best
    mechanisms in modern board gaming, the character variety is unmatched, and a
    campaign played through with the same group is a genuinely memorable
    experience.
  </p>

  <p>
    But it is a commitment, not a purchase. Buy it if you have the group, the
    evenings and the shelf. If any of those three is uncertain, start smaller —
    the game will still be there when you're sure.
  </p>
</Article>
